@spec YEP

# yep streams forever by design, so only invocations that terminate on their
# own can be pinned here. The invalid-UTF-8 rejection happens at argument
# parsing and exits immediately, so it has expected values an author can
# write down: exit code 1 and an error on stderr that names the problem.
#
# The streaming behaviors stay in ordinary test tasks with verify commands,
# because checking them needs a bounding consumer or a pipe:
# - default output ("y" repeatedly) and custom strings: yep | head -n 3
# - multiple args joined with one space: yep foo bar | head -n 1
# - empty string producing blank lines: yep "" | head -n 3
# - broken pipe exiting 0 silently: yep | head -n 1; check exit status
# - throughput over 1 GB/s: a timing harness, not fixed expected values
# Tag those tasks with covers = ["Output Repeated String"] in plan.toml so
# the coverage ledger counts them.

# Three distinct ways bytes can fail UTF-8 validation: bytes that are never
# valid anywhere (0xff, 0xfe), a lone continuation byte (0x80), and a lead
# byte with a broken continuation (0xc3 0x28). The last scenario also checks
# that validation covers every argument, not just the first.

@covers "Output Repeated String"
scenario rejects bytes that are never valid utf-8:
when $ yep \xff\xfe
then exit 1
then stderr has "UTF-8"

@covers "Output Repeated String"
scenario rejects a lone continuation byte:
when $ yep \x80
then exit 1
then stderr has "UTF-8"

@covers "Output Repeated String"
scenario rejects invalid utf-8 in any argument:
when $ yep hello \xc3\x28
then exit 1
then stderr has "UTF-8"
